 "conclusions": [
  "Areas, Pulse and Stay are local language surfaces first. The same question asked in the local language about a local city returns between ten and forty times the volume of the English phrasing about a foreign city: 6,700 against 150 for events today, 1,600 against nothing for rooms, 2,900 against 150 for coliving. An English taxonomy translated outward would have missed almost all of this, and a market gate that enumerates these families only where they were measured in English would have been wrong.",
  "Commercial value and search volume point in different directions, and the monetisation map has to carry both. Hotels near Madison Square Garden is 3,000 a month at difficulty 2 with a cost per click of one dollar forty. Concerts in Chicago is 13,000 a month at difficulty 19 with a cost per click of three cents. The second has four times the volume and a fraction of the value.",
  "Tools are the largest single opportunity measured anywhere in this research, and the winnable ones are the narrow ones. Tax calculator is 351,000 a month at difficulty 74 and salary calculator 148,000 at 69, which are not winnable from a standing start. Where should I live quiz is 1,500 at difficulty 1, moving cost calculator is 2,500 at difficulty 41 with a five dollar cost per click, and commute calculator is 350 at difficulty 9 with a two dollar cost per click.",
  "Several phrasings the brief assumed are dead in English and have to be recorded as such: cafes in a named neighbourhood, where to stay for a named race, restaurants near a named festival, roommates in a named city, and tech networking events in a named city all returned nothing. The intents behind them are real; these exact phrasings are not how they are searched."
 ]
